  EnvironmentalTopicDocument(
    number: 25,
    title: '''Air Quality Management''',
    definition: '''Air quality management is the planned prevention, measurement and control of airborne pollutants generated by project activities. It covers dust, combustion exhaust, process emissions, fumes, vapours and other contaminants that may affect workers, neighbouring communities or the environment. The Environmental Officer identifies sources and sensitive receptors, evaluates pathways, and selects controls proportionate to the activity and applicable approvals.''',
    purpose: '''The objective is to prevent avoidable emissions, protect people and ecosystems, meet permit conditions, and provide traceable evidence that controls remain effective. Air management should be integrated into method statements, procurement, logistics, maintenance and daily site supervision.''',
    regulatoryBasis: '''Review applicable UAE federal environmental legislation, the competent emirate authority’s requirements, project environmental approval, EIA commitments and permit conditions. Abu Dhabi and Dubai requirements may differ by activity and location. Confirm the current instrument and exact applicability before quoting a limit; international guidance is supplementary unless adopted by an approval or contract.''',
    procedure: '''Prepare an emissions-source inventory before work begins. Map sources and receptors; assess wind direction, proximity and operating hours; select source reduction, enclosure, extraction, wet suppression or scheduling controls; assign owners; inspect controls before startup; monitor when required; investigate complaints and abnormal readings; document corrective actions and verify close-out.''',
    documents: '''Environmental Management Plan; air-emissions/aspects register; permit and approval register; monitoring plan and reports; equipment maintenance records; complaint log; inspection checklists; corrective-action register. Mark each item as authority-required, permit-conditioned, project-required or recommended after checking the actual approval.''',
    documentOwnership: '''The Environmental Officer maintains the register and monitoring schedule; construction and plant managers implement controls; competent monitoring providers conduct specified measurements; the Project Manager ensures resources; the designated approver signs submissions where required.''',
    verification: '''Verify source locations against the approved layout, inspect control condition during operation, review monitoring method and calibration, compare results only with applicable criteria, check wind and activity logs, and trace exceedances to corrective-action evidence.''',
    commonErrors: '''Treating one dust observation as proof of compliance; monitoring at an unrepresentative location; relying on PPE instead of source control; using expired or unsuitable calibration; failing to investigate repeated complaints.''',
    fieldExample: '''During bulk earthworks near occupied premises, the team phases excavation, covers stockpiles, uses suitable suppression, limits unnecessary idling, checks downwind conditions and records inspections. If dust escapes the work boundary, pause the dusty task, correct the source and record verification.''',
    checklist: '''Checklist: sources/receptors identified; approval conditions reviewed; controls installed; responsible person assigned; monitoring locations and triggers defined; equipment fit for purpose; complaints route active; records reviewed; corrective actions closed. Interview: explain how you would control and investigate dust or exhaust impacts without assuming an unverified legal threshold.''',
    interview: '''Interview preparation: Define air quality management in your own words; identify the applicable permit or approved plan; explain the source–pathway–receptor relationship; describe prevention, monitoring, documentation and escalation steps; and give a site example. A strong answer distinguishes verified legal obligations from project controls and explains how effectiveness is checked.''',
