  EnvironmentalTopicDocument(
    number: 26,
    title: '''Construction Dust Control''',
    definition: '''Construction dust is airborne particulate matter released by excavation, grading, cutting, crushing, vehicle movement, material handling and exposed soil. Fine particles can travel beyond the work area, reduce visibility, soil nearby property and affect respiratory health. Dust control combines prevention at source, containment, housekeeping and monitoring.''',
    purpose: '''The purpose is to minimize dust generation and migration, protect workers and neighbours, maintain safe visibility and demonstrate compliance with site-specific approval conditions. Controls must be selected for the material, weather, task and proximity of receptors.''',
    regulatoryBasis: '''Apply the project EMP, applicable authority approvals and occupational exposure requirements where worker exposure is involved. Confirm any site boundary, particulate or nuisance criteria from current permits or authority directions; do not substitute generic values for project limits.''',
    procedure: '''Before work, identify dusty tasks and receptors; stabilize access roads; cover or appropriately manage stockpiles; use suitable wet methods or local extraction; control drop heights; cover loads; clean wheels and paved routes; stop or modify work when controls cannot prevent off-site migration; inspect after wind or process changes.''',
    documents: '''Dust management procedure; daily dust inspection; water-use log; vehicle/load covering records; monitoring reports if required; complaint and response log; maintenance records; toolbox-talk attendance; corrective-action evidence.''',
    documentOwnership: '''The earthworks supervisor runs task controls; logistics controls haulage; the Environmental Officer sets inspection and monitoring requirements; subcontractors maintain their work fronts; the site manager authorizes resources and escalation.''',
    verification: '''Walk the boundary and downwind receptors during active work; inspect suppression nozzles and water supply; confirm water use does not create runoff; review weather and activity records; document time, location, source, action and follow-up.''',
    commonErrors: '''Over-wetting and creating contaminated runoff; assuming water spraying alone controls fine dust; leaving stockpiles uncovered; allowing vehicle speeds and road conditions to go uncontrolled; closing complaints without field verification.''',
    fieldExample: '''A concrete cutting crew uses suitable wet cutting or extraction, isolates the area, collects slurry rather than washing it into drains, checks adjacent receptors and records the inspection. If visible dust crosses the boundary, stop and improve controls before restarting.''',
    checklist: '''Checklist: task assessed; sensitive receptors identified; suppression/extraction available; loads covered; roads maintained; runoff controlled; boundary inspected; complaints logged; restart authorized after correction. Interview: describe dust hierarchy and how you avoid transferring an air problem into a water-pollution problem.''',
    interview: '''Interview preparation: Define construction dust control in your own words; identify the applicable permit or approved plan; explain the source–pathway–receptor relationship; describe prevention, monitoring, documentation and escalation steps; and give a site example. A strong answer distinguishes verified legal obligations from project controls and explains how effectiveness is checked.''',
  ),
